{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,27]],"date-time":"2026-08-27T17:06:01Z","timestamp":1787850361102,"version":"build-2784847793"},"reference-count":157,"publisher":"Annual Reviews","issue":"1","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,26]],"date-time":"2024-09-26T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1727308800000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":[],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,26]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>It has been 5 years since the data sample from the LHCb detector, the first experiment optimized for heavy-flavor physics studies at a hadronic collider, was completed. These data led to many major discoveries in exotic hadron spectroscopy, which we review in this article. We supplement the experimental results with a selection of phenomenological interpretations. As the upgraded LHCb detector is expected to collect a larger data sample starting in 2024, we also discuss the potential of the LHCb program in exotic hadron physics for the near future and beyond.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1146\/annurev-nucl-102422-040628","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,10]],"date-time":"2024-07-10T16:40:13Z","timestamp":1720629613000},"page":"583-612","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":20,"title":["Exotic Hadrons at LHCb"],"prefix":"10.1146","volume":"74","author":[{"given":"Daniel","family":"Johnson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"1School of Physics and Astronomy, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, United Kingdom; email:\u00a0daniel.johnson@cern.ch"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ivan","family":"Polyakov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"2European Organization for Nuclear Research (CERN), Geneva, Switzerland; email:\u00a0ivan.polyakov@cern.ch"}],"role":[{"vocabula